(and decent the widow was in all her ways; and so when I couldn?t stand) Tj
T* (it no longer I lit out. I got into my old rags and my sugar-hogshead) Tj
T* (again, and was free and satisfied. But Tom Sawyer he hunted me up and) Tj
T* (said he was going to start a band of robbers, and I might join if I) Tj
T* (would go back to the widow and be respectable. So I went back.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The widow she cried over me, and called me a poor lost lamb, and she) Tj
T* (called me a lot of other names, too, but she never meant no harm by it.) Tj
T* (She put me in them new clothes again, and I couldn?t do nothing but) Tj
T* (sweat and sweat, and feel all cramped up. Well, then, the old thing) Tj
T* (commenced again. The widow rung a bell for supper, and you had to come) Tj
T* (to time. When you got to the table you couldn?t go right to eating, but) Tj
T* (you had to wait for the widow to tuck down her head and grumble a) Tj
T* (little over the victuals, though there warn?t really anything the) Tj
T* (matter with them,?that is, nothing only everything was cooked by) Tj
T* (itself. In a barrel of odds and ends it is different; things get mixed) Tj
T* (up, and the juice kind of swaps around, and the things go better.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (After supper she got out her book and learned me about Moses and the) Tj
T* (Bulrushers, and I was in a sweat to find out all about him; but) Tj
T* (by-and-by she let it out that Moses had been dead a considerable long) Tj
T* (time; so then I didn?t care no more about him, because I don?t take no) Tj
T* (stock in dead people.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Pretty soon I wanted to smoke, and asked the widow to let me. But she) Tj
T* (wouldn?t. She said it was a mean practice and wasn?t clean, and I must) Tj
T* (try to not do it any more. That is just the way with some people. They) Tj
T* (get down on a thing when they don?t know nothing about it. Here she was) Tj
T* (a-bothering about Moses, which was no kin to her, and no use to) Tj
T* (anybody, being gone, you see, yet finding a power of fault with me for) Tj
T* (doing a thing that had some good in it. And she took snuff, too; of) Tj
T* (course that was all right, because she done it herself.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Her sister, Miss Watson, a tolerable slim old maid, with goggles on,) Tj
T* (had just come to live with her, and took a set at me now with a) Tj
T* (spelling-book. She worked me middling hard for about an hour, and then) Tj
T* (the widow made her ease up. I couldn?t stood it much longer. Then for) Tj
T* (an hour it was deadly dull, and I was fidgety. Miss Watson would say,) Tj
T* (?Don?t put your feet up there, Huckleberry;? and ?Don?t scrunch up like) Tj
T* (that, Huckleberry?set up straight;? and pretty soon she would say,) Tj
T* (?Don?t gap and stretch like that, Huckleberry?why don?t you try to) Tj
